\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Graph codes land in the resistance completion}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Label the corners of #{V_0} in the
\vocabk{gasket graph}{fdip-00DP} by #{1,2,3}. The subcell
named by a letter is the child triangle containing the
corner of that letter. An infinite word #{\omega} thus
names a nested sequence of graph cells. Let #{X} be the
resistance completion in \ref{fdip-00EN}, and let
#{\pi_X(\omega)} be the unique point common to the closures
of those cells. Then #{\pi_X} is continuous and surjective
onto #{X}. The paired junction words of \ref{fdip-00EO}
are sent to the same vertex.}

\proof{
\p{Every vertex of a child cell is a vertex of the parent
cell. \ref{fdip-00EM} bounds the resistance diameter of the
vertices in an #{n}-cell by #{4\bigl(\frac{3}{5}\bigr)^n}.
The closures in the complete space #{X} are nested and their
diameters tend to #{0}, so they meet in one point. Words
with the same prefix of length #{n} land in one cell, and
#{\pi_X} is continuous. The vertices of a cell are the union
of the vertices of its three subcells, so the closure of
the cell is the union of the three subcell closures. The
#{0}-cell is all of #{V_*}, and its closure is #{X}. Every
point of #{X} therefore lies on some nested sequence, and
#{\pi_X} is surjective.}

\p{Take the paired junction words of \ref{fdip-00EO}, with
letters #{i\ne j} after a common prefix. At the first
differing place they enter the two subcells that contain
the midpoint of the edge joining those corners. In each
subcell that midpoint is the corner named by the constant
letter which follows, so every later child contains the
midpoint. Both images equal that vertex of #{V_*}.}
}
